The Security Council,

Recalling all its previous relevant resolutions and statements of its President concerning Yemen, including resolutions 2140 (2014), 2216 (2015), 2451 (2018), 2452 (2019), 2624 (2022), 2664 (2022), 2707 (2023), 2722 (2024), and 2758 (2024),

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the unity, sovereignty, independence, and territorial integrity of Yemen,

Expressing grave concern at the continued conflict and instability in Yemen, as well as the significant ongoing humanitarian challenges faced by the civilian population,

Expressing serious concern at the ongoing detention by the Houthis of United Nations personnel, personnel of international and non-governmental organisations, and diplomatic personnel, and demanding their immediate, safe, and unconditional release,

Taking note of the final report of the Panel of Experts on Yemen (S/2025/650),

Determining that the situation in Yemen continues to constitute a threat to international peace and security,

Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,

1. Decides to renew until 15 November 2026 the measures imposed by paragraphs 11 and 15 of resolution 2140 (2014), reaffirms the provisions of paragraphs 12, 13, 14 and 16 of resolution 2140 (2014), and further reaffirms the provisions of paragraphs 14 to 17 of resolution 2216 (2015);

2. Emphasises the importance of facilitating humanitarian assistance and commercial imports of essential goods to meet civilians’ basic survival needs, and reaffirms its decision in resolution 2664 (2022);

3. Decides to extend until 15 December 2026 the mandate of the Panel of Experts as contained in paragraph 21 of resolution 2140 (2014) and paragraph 21 of resolution 2216 (2015), and expresses its intention to review the mandate and take appropriate action regarding the further extension no later than 15 November 2026;

4. Requests the Panel of Experts to provide a midterm update to the Committee established pursuant to paragraph 19 of resolution 2140 (2014) (“the Committee”) no later than 15 April 2026, and to submit a final report to the Security Council no later than 15 October 2026, after discussion with the Committee;

5. Directs the Panel of Experts to cooperate with other relevant expert groups established by the Security Council to support the work of its sanctions committees, in particular the Analytical Support and Sanctions Monitoring Team established pursuant to resolutions 1526 (2004) and 2253 (2015);

6. Urges all parties and all Member States, as well as international, regional, and subregional organisations, to ensure full cooperation with the Panel of Experts, and further urges all Member States involved to ensure the safety of the members of the Panel of Experts and unhindered access to persons, documents, and sites in order for the Panel to execute its mandate;

7. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

The Security Council,

